# Hook rules

## Event types

```go
hooks.PreToolUse         // before tool runs — may block by returning error
hooks.PostToolUse        // after success — best-effort, non-fatal
hooks.PostToolUseFailure // after tool error — best-effort, non-fatal
hooks.SessionStart
hooks.SessionEnd
```

## Error behavior

| Hook | Non-nil return |
|------|----------------|
| `PreToolUse` | Blocks the tool; orchestrator surfaces as `is_error` result |
| `PostToolUse` / `PostToolUseFailure` | Logged with `slog.WarnContext`; loop continues |
| `SessionStart` / `SessionEnd` | Logged; session continues |

## Register in `chat_wiring.go`, not `main.go`

```go
hookReg := hooks.New()
hookReg.On(hooks.PreToolUse, myHandler)
```

## PostToolUseFailure — two failure kinds

- `hooks.FailureExecuteError` — `Tool.Execute` returned a non-nil Go error.
- `hooks.FailureErrorResult` — Execute succeeded but `Result.IsError == true`.

Both are propagated as JSON field `failure_kind` in subprocess/HTTP hooks (`hooks/wire.go`).

## Do not block in PostToolUse

PostToolUse handlers must be fast and non-blocking. Side effects (logging, metrics, memory writes) are fine; HTTP calls or disk I/O that could fail should be done asynchronously or skipped if the context is cancelled.
